<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dol-de-Bretagne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dol-de-Bretagne is a town in north-eastern Brittany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, near the Bay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mont-saint-michel">Mont-Saint-Michel</a>. By tradition it grew from a monastery founded by the Welsh saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samson">Samson</a> in the 6th century, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, from the 9th century until 1199, claimed to be archbishops of all Brittany. The bishopric was abolished in 1790, but its Gothic cathedral of Saint Samson survives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dol-de-bretagne.jpg" alt="Dol-de-Bretagne" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LimoWreck · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dol-de-Bretagne_-_La_Grande_Rue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
